=== the same surface in USD, with token capex amortised over a reuse count N ===
  CPU $1.389e-05/core-s. Seconds and dollars are proportional while nothing calls an
  LLM at solve time, so the CURVE SHAPE is unchanged -- what dollars add is commensurability with
  the token cost of producing the operator, which seconds cannot express.

  system      S_inf  B_half ($)  $/solve at S_inf/2  $ for a full pass
  off_150        12      0.0079              0.0013             0.0415
  off_400        12      0.0083              0.0014             0.0766
  on_400         22      0.0216              0.0020             0.0933

  effective $/solve once the operator's INVENTION capex is amortised over N solves
  (marginal $/solve: off_150 0.01276, on_400 0.01696)
                       capex        N=1       N=22      N=100     N=1000  breakeven N
    $0.08  (1 agent episode)     0.0970     0.0206     0.0178     0.0170            5
         $0.40  (5 episodes)     0.4170     0.0351     0.0210     0.0174           24
        $2.00  (25 episodes)     2.0170     0.1079     0.0370     0.0190          118
       $20.00 (250 episodes)    20.0170     0.9260     0.2170     0.0370         1180
  N = 22 is one gated pass over the 60-target benchmark, so the right-hand column is also
  'how many full benchmark passes the invention cost is worth'. A single agent episode ($0.08)
  costs about as much as ONE ENTIRE PASS ($0.093) -- at this scale CPU is nearly free and the
  token spend of the R&D loop, not of solving, is the dominant cost.

  when do RUNTIME tokens (an LLM inside the solve loop) start to matter?
    one LLM call per attempt         +$0.00175/attempt = 112.6% on top of $0.00155
    one per target (~8 attempts)     +$0.00022/attempt =  14.1% on top of $0.00155
    one per benchmark pass           +$0.00003/attempt =   1.9% on top of $0.00155
    one LLM call at 2k/0.5k costs $0.00175, about the same as one solve ATTEMPT ($0.00155),
    and one at 20k/4k costs $0.016, about the same as one finished SOLVE ($0.017).

